# Adds `provision-model` to super-stt-cli, 2026-10-26.
#
# Why this exists: dictation needs a speech model loaded before it does
# anything, and upstream's ONLY documented way to install one is "Open the
# app, go to Library -> Browse" — the app being super-stt-app, a libcosmic
# GUI we deliberately don't build (see package.nix's WORKSPACE SUBSET
# note). Without this patch, every host needs a human to manually curl the
# daemon's HTTP API after reading its source to work out the endpoints —
# not declarative, not something another fleet member could reproduce.
#
# Why the CLI, not a plain curl/script: the daemon's `/auth/request` only
# auto-approves ("first-party") for a request from a binary named exactly
# super-stt-cli/-app/-cosmic-applet, co-located with the daemon binary
# (`is_official_client` in super-stt-daemon's consent.rs checks
# /proc/<pid>/exe). Anything else — curl, a standalone script — hits a
# real human-consent popup, which doesn't exist to click in a headless
# systemd unit. Patching the CLI we already build and already trust is the
# only path that stays inside that allowlist.
#
# What it does: `stt provision-model` installs this fleet's default local
# model's backend, selects it for the transcription stage, loads the
# model, and is idempotent (checks first, does nothing if already loaded)
# — safe to wire as a systemd oneshot that runs after the daemon starts on
# every boot. Uses its own AppId ("super-stt-cli-provision") with the
# `settings` scope, separate from the AppId ping/status/record/stop share
# (scoped transcribe+status), so the two don't fight over one cached
# token's scopes — see the const's doc comment in the diff below.
#
# Endpoints driven (all require the `settings` scope; see
# docs/protocol/endpoints/v1/ in the super-stt repo for the full contract):
#   POST /registry/backend/install  { "source": "<backend repo id>" }
#   GET  /backend/list                                 (poll until installed)
#   POST /pipeline/{stage}                { "source": "<backend repo id>" }
#   POST /pipeline/{stage}/model           { "model": "<model name>" }
#   GET  /pipeline/{stage}/model                        (poll until loaded)
#
# The default model (github.com/jorge-menjivar/super-stt-whisper,
# whisper-small.en) is picked for being CPU-capable — no GPU required, so
# it works on every host in the fleet — and `online: false` in the
# published catalog, matching this fleet's "nothing leaves the machine"
# stance for dictation (docs/user/keyboard.md). Not the most accurate
# option available; the most broadly compatible one.
#
# TOKEN_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT (added after the first real run, same day): the
# session token mint goes through the system keyring/secret-service over
# D-Bus, and a stuck/locked keyring hangs that call forever with zero
# error output — the first attempt on a real host burned a full 30
# minutes (systemd's own TimeoutStartSec) before being force-killed,
# having printed nothing at all. Traced to a real gisnix gap: COSMIC has
# no native keyring unlock prompter, and the gcr package that's supposed
# to provide one was only on PATH, never registered as a D-Bus service
# (see software/desktop/environments/cosmic/ssh-gpg.nix's
# services.dbus.packages fix, same day) — so a mid-session unlock
# request had nothing to answer it. This timeout doesn't fix that; it
# just makes the failure fast and loud instead of a silent half-hour
# stall on every single attempt.
